# Patch: supervisor: host resolvers in the DHCP lease + power-off stop
# Source:  https://github.com/EvilBorsch/droidloom/commit/2712d37c58d38ef4f1d4586ef07e5d1596ea7246
# Author:  EvilBorsch <44337169+EvilBorsch@users.noreply.github.com>
# Date:    2026-11-22T10:23:16Z
# Why:     Root cause 2 of our cell-offline bug (#48 secondary): DHCP lease hardcodes
#          dns:[1.1.1.1, 8.8.8.8]. Our campus network forwards UDP/53 ONLY to the
#          on-campus resolver; 1.1.1.1/8.8.8.8 timeout => Android marks SSID
#          NO_INTERNET_PERMANENT. This patch reads host's /run/systemd/resolve/resolv.conf
#          or /etc/resolv.conf and puts those resolvers first in the lease.
#          Also changes stop sequence: SIGTERM -> power-off (CAP_SYS_BOOT makes init
#          ignore SIGTERM; setprop sys.powerctl=shutdown is correct).
# Depends: Must be applied AFTER 002-remove-forward-signals.patch (context in
#          development.rs assumes 5380af3's signal_namespace_init() is present).
# Retire:  When cell's `dumpsys connectivity` shows DNS != 1.1.1.1/8.8.8.8 without
#          manual nft redirect (i.e., upstream lease reads host resolvers).
#          Check: `strings /usr/bin/droidloom-supervisor | grep -c resolv.conf` > 0.
From 2712d37c58d38ef4f1d4586ef07e5d1596ea7246 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: EvilBorsch <44337169+EvilBorsch@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 13:23:16 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] supervisor: host resolvers in the lease, stop Android by
 powering it off

Two faults seen on a OnePlus Pad 3 running Ubuntu 26.04:

The DHCP lease named only 1.1.1.1 and 8.8.8.8. The tablet's network drops
plain DNS to both (its provider answers only from its own resolvers),
though DNS over TLS to them works. Android's first Internet check runs
before its encrypted DNS is up, so both probes timed out after 12.5 s
(NetworkMonitor: PROBE_DNS ... FAIL ... Timeout), the saved network became
NETWORK_SELECTION_DISABLED_NO_INTERNET_PERMANENT, and from the next start
Android no longer joined it: apps were offline. The lease now lists the
resolvers the host's network gave the host (systemd-resolved's upstream
list, else /etc/resolv.conf; IPv4, not loopback; at most two) before the
public ones, four at most.

Stopping took 14 s: Android's init has CAP_SYS_BOOT in the cell, so it
installs no SIGTERM handler, and the kernel drops a signal that a PID
namespace's init does not handle. The SIGTERM of the previous commit, like
the one unshare --forward-signals would have passed on, did nothing, and
stop() ran into its 10 s deadline and killed the owner. Ask Android to
power off instead (setprop sys.powerctl shutdown through nsenter, as the
other control commands run): init shuts down and its reboot call ends the
PID namespace. Done by hand on the tablet, Android's init was gone 337 ms
after the request.

The supervisor's library tests pass (143, two new). Not yet run on the
tablet as a built runtime.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
